    "app.torm.que.c": "<ul><li><b>Satellite, not radar.</b> Every 10 minutes the GOES-19 satellite measures the temperature of the top of the clouds. The coldest are very tall clouds: the ones that usually bring downpours and lightning.</li><li><b>It can be wrong.</b> It can rain hard under a cloud that does not look cold, and a cold cloud can pass without rain. The image arrives 20 to 40 minutes late.</li><li><b>Lightning:</b> detected by the same satellite (GLM sensor). The last half hour is shown.</li><li><b>Rain in the next hour:</b> it is the Open-Meteo model, not a measurement.</li><li><b>Not official.</b> Official alerts come from the SNGR and INAMHI. If the water rises, follow «My place» and call 911.</li></ul><p class=\"mt-2 text-gris\">Sources: NOAA GOES-19 (images through NASA GIBS), GLM through Unidata THREDDS (UCAR) and Open-Meteo. Our server fetches them: your phone does not connect to them.</p>",
